# /etc/default/waydesk: settings of the waydesk services.

# Where the RDP port listens. Loopback by default: reach it through an SSH tunnel or a VPN. Put a VPN or private
# address here to serve a network; do not publish it to the internet without reading docs/MULTIUSER-DESIGN.md.
WAYDESK_BIND=127.0.0.1:3389

# Which source addresses may connect: checked when a connection is accepted, before TLS and before any login (a daemon on loopback needs none).
# Without a line here a daemon on any other address allows only local networks (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 100.64/10, link-local, fc00::/7).
# Ranges narrow it: WAYDESK_ALLOW_FROM="192.168.1.0/24 10.8.0.0/24" (also single addresses, and the words private, loopback and any).
# "any" allows every address: then keep the port off the open internet (a password login, no second factor). `waydesk-admin access` sets this
# and WAYDESK_BIND together.
#WAYDESK_ALLOW_FROM=

# Extra options of the network daemon (see the README): --quality must be the same here and in the session args.
WAYDESK_ARGS=

# Options of the sessions the helper starts, for example: --idle-timeout 28800 --end-on-disconnect --kb-layout pt
#   --idle-timeout SECONDS   a session without a client ends after this long (default 28800 = 8 h, 0 = never)
#   --end-on-disconnect      a session ends as soon as its client leaves
#   --bitrate BITS           H.264 bitrate (default 40000000); lower it for slow links, with --quality MAXQP
WAYDESK_SESSION_ARGS=

# Where the mouse pointer is drawn: "client" (default) = by the RDP client, which is instant but always an arrow (sway gets a
# cursor theme with nothing in it, waydesk-blank); "server" = in the picture, with the shape the app asked for (text cursor,
# resize arrows), but a network round trip behind, and only for clients that make their own pointer invisible when told to.
#WAYDESK_CURSOR=client

# Who may log in: members of the group waydesk-users (the package makes it and, on a first install, adds the account that ran the
# installation). Add accounts with: adduser ACCOUNT waydesk-users. To let every ordinary account with a login shell in, as
# before, put the line below WITH nothing after the equals sign; another group: WAYDESK_HELPER_ARGS=--require-group NAME
#WAYDESK_HELPER_ARGS=--require-group waydesk-users

# Memory: a session costs about 0.5 GB for the desktop itself (see the README, "Sizing"), most of it the shell (DankMaterialShell).
# 1 makes the shell use Qt's software renderer: about 110 MB less per session (249 MB against 360 MB resident, DMS 2.0). Elements cannot be
# rounded or shaded then, so new accounts start with square corners; some previews stay blank.
#WAYDESK_DMS_SOFTWARE=1

# A new session is refused when the machine cannot take one more (the account's running session is never refused: it reconnects).
# By default it needs the larger of 900 MB and 15 % of the memory available (`waydesk-admin status` shows it and whether a login would
# be accepted now). Change it, or limit the accounts that may have a session, by adding to the helper's options (keep the group):
#WAYDESK_HELPER_ARGS=--require-group waydesk-users --min-available-mb 1500 --max-active-users 6
#   --min-available-mb N    0 switches the memory check off
#   --max-active-users N    0 (the default) is no limit

# A client far away (about 100 ms: another country over the internet). The picture can only go as fast as frames in flight divided by the round
# trip, so allow more frames in the air than the 3 that suit a local network (1 to 32), and take the bitrate and the quality ceiling down so that
# a full-screen change is a smaller burst. --bitrate and --quality must be given to the daemon (WAYDESK_ARGS) AND to the sessions
# (WAYDESK_SESSION_ARGS): the session is what encodes, the daemon only tells the client the quality. Restart waydesk, and end the sessions
# (or log out) so that they start again. Over an SSH tunnel TCP runs inside TCP and stalls at this distance: a WireGuard VPN is better
# (docs/WIREGUARD.md). Measured with 97 ms: 3 frames in flight over an SSH tunnel was slow, 8 over WireGuard much better, and what stays is
# the round trip itself (typing and moving windows feel it; the pointer does not, the client draws it).
#WAYDESK_FRAMES_IN_FLIGHT=8
#WAYDESK_ARGS="--bitrate 10000000 --quality 28"
#WAYDESK_SESSION_ARGS="--bitrate 10000000 --quality 28"

# Video encoder: "screen" (default) uses OpenH264's screen-content mode, which needs about half the bandwidth for moving pictures but
# about two to three times the CPU of "camera" (measured with a full-screen 720p30 test pattern: 26-29 % against 42-45 % of a core
# for a worst-case pattern but 3x for a mostly still desktop, which camera mode encodes almost for free; see docs/MEASUREMENTS.md).
# The encoder runs at low complexity (WAYDESK_H264_COMPLEXITY=medium|high to change it). On a server with few cores that plays video, "camera" can
# keep the frame rate up. Takes effect for sessions that start after the change (end the session or log out first).
#WAYDESK_H264_USAGE=camera
